  "worked_solution": "To apply the ratio test, we define ${a}_{n}=\\frac{(-1)^{n}{x}^{2n+1}}{(2n+1)!}.$ Then, we calculate the ratio of two successive terms and take the limit as $n\\to \\infty :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}{x}^{2n+3}}{(2n+3)!}\\cdot \\frac{(2n+1)!}{(-1)^{n}{x}^{2n+1}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{(-1){x}^{2}}{(2n+3)(2n+2)}| \\\\  & =|-{x}^{2}|{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{(2n+3)(2n+2)} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe series converges if $L<1.$ In this case, the convergence condition is satisfied for all $-\\infty <x<\\infty .$ Therefore, we conclude that the radius of convergence is $R=\\infty ,$ and the interval of convergence is $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
